In article <1993Sep15.153445@IASTATE.EDU> phaedrus@IASTATE.EDU (James R. Goodfriend) writes:
>
>	It just occurred to me (haven't tried it yet) to have my pet steal
>everything from a shop, then sell it back, then have the pet steal it again,
>then sell it back, forever and ever.  Sure it'd be slow, and there'd be
>a food problem, but can anybody support or refute this idea?

Shopkeepers have a limited amount of cash - on the order of a few thousand
gold pieces.  After they have given you all they have, they start to offer
credit, good on purchases from their store.  With a well-trained pet, it
doesn't take too long to clean out a shop if it has things of reasonable
value to steal and sell back.  I've only done it once, just to see what
would happen.  It's too much work for a relatively small return.  Just kill
him for his gold, if you want it that badly.  (Of course, if you're not
chaotic, that has a price also.  And, you need some way to kill him, which
is a problem for most beginning characters.)

